<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  VelChat transport security policy (VC-018, §A1/§D4). ADR: docs/adr/0009-certificate-pinning.md

  This file is the WHOLE pinning mechanism on Android. The platform enforces it for every TLS
  socket the process opens, which is why it was chosen over an OkHttp CertificatePinner: it also
  covers the raw HttpURLConnection that `push/PushAckClient.kt` uses to send a delivery receipt
  with no React context alive, which a CertificatePinner installed into React Native's OkHttp
  client would never see.

  ** THE PIN-SETS BELOW ARE EMPTY ON PURPOSE. NOTHING IS PINNED YET. **

  An empty <pin-set> is not enforced by the platform (NetworkSecurityTrustManager returns early
  when the set is empty), so the app behaves today exactly as it did before this file existed,
  minus the cleartext narrowing described further down. That is the deliberate outcome: a pin can
  only be computed from the live certificate, and a WRONG pin brands every install that carries
  it permanently unable to reach the backend — no server-side change can rescue it, because the
  client aborts the handshake before it ever speaks to us. A mechanism with no pins is safe; a
  guessed pin is not recoverable.

  HOW TO FILL ONE IN
    openssl s_client -servername <host> -connect <host>:443 -showcerts </dev/null \
      | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout \
      | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
      | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl enc -base64
  Run it once per certificate you want to accept and paste the base64 into a <pin> below.

  TWO RULES, BOTH ENFORCED BY src/infra/native/__tests__/transportSecurity.test.ts
    1. Never ship a single pin. Ship at least two: the key in use, plus a BACKUP whose private
       key is held offline and is not yet deployed (or the issuing CA's key). One pin plus one
       routine certificate rotation is how an app bricks itself.
    2. Always set `expiration` on the pin-set. Past that date the platform stops enforcing pins
       instead of failing every connection — the only fail-open valve available, and the last
       thing standing between a forgotten rotation and a dead install base. Pick a date you will
       actually still be shipping updates before, e.g. 6-12 months out.

  A pin shape (do NOT uncomment: the digests are placeholders, not real keys):
    <pin-set expiration="2027-06-30">
      <pin digest="SHA-256">BASE64_OF_THE_LIVE_LEAF_OR_INTERMEDIATE_SPKI=</pin>
      <pin digest="SHA-256">BASE64_OF_THE_OFFLINE_BACKUP_SPKI=</pin>
    </pin-set>

  Before pinning prod, settle WHAT is being pinned. `velchat.duckdns.org` is served by Caddy with
  Let's Encrypt, which generates a fresh key on every renewal by default — pinning that leaf
  means a dead app roughly every 60 days. Either pin the issuing CA's SPKI, or have the backend
  commit to a fixed key (Caddy `key_type` + a reused key) and a published backup. That decision
  belongs to whoever owns the VM, not to this file.
-->
<network-security-config>

  <!--
    Default for every host that has no entry of its own: system CAs only, no cleartext.

    NOTE this supersedes `android:usesCleartextTraffic` in the manifest entirely — once a
    network security config exists, the manifest attribute is ignored on every API level this
    app supports (minSdk 24). So the cleartext behaviour the debug builds rely on has to be
    restated here; that is what the loopback block below is for.
  -->
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>

  <!--
    The local development loop, and nothing else.

    `pnpm android` reaches Metro and a locally-run backend over plain http: the emulator sees the
    host machine as 10.0.2.2, a USB device sees it as localhost via `adb reverse`. Denying
    cleartext here would break `pnpm start` / `pnpm reverse` on the first request, which is why
    these four are listed rather than left to the base config.

    This block is NOT limited to debug builds — cleartext rules cannot live in
    <debug-overrides>, and a per-buildType config would need a file under `src/debug/res/xml/`.
    The exposure it leaves in a release APK is cleartext to loopback and to the emulator's host
    alias, neither of which is reachable from a network. It is still strictly narrower than the
    `usesCleartextTraffic=true` that debug builds carried before this file existed.

    If you run Metro over a LAN address instead (a physical device without `adb reverse`), add
    that IP here or the bundle will not load.
  -->
  <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    <!-- The Android emulator's alias for the host machine. -->
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    <!-- Genymotion's equivalent. -->
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.3.2</domain>
  </domain-config>

  <!--
    One entry per origin any flavor can be built against, taken from .env.dev / .env.stage /
    .env.prod. Hosts are matched EXACTLY (no includeSubdomains): a host that is missing from this
    file is silently unpinned even once pins land elsewhere, and that is the quiet hole the test
    exists to close. When you add or change a URL in a .env file, add it here in the same commit.
  -->

  <!-- prod (.env.prod) — Azure VM behind Caddy + Let's Encrypt; REST and WS share the origin. -->
  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">velchat.duckdns.org</domain>
    <!-- No <pin-set> until a real pin exists: an EMPTY one enforces nothing at runtime and
         Android lint treats it as a fatal error in a release build, so the app could not be
         assembled at all. Paste the pin-set from the header recipe here when the pins land. -->
  </domain-config>

  <!-- dev (.env.dev) — Render, topology axis6. WS deliberately bypasses the edge gateway. -->
  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">velchat-edge-gateway-2aje.onrender.com</domain>
    <!-- No <pin-set> until a real pin exists: an EMPTY one enforces nothing at runtime and
         Android lint treats it as a fatal error in a release build, so the app could not be
         assembled at all. Paste the pin-set from the header recipe here when the pins land. -->
  </domain-config>
  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">velchat-realtime-service-2aje.onrender.com</domain>
    <!-- No <pin-set> until a real pin exists: an EMPTY one enforces nothing at runtime and
         Android lint treats it as a fatal error in a release build, so the app could not be
         assembled at all. Paste the pin-set from the header recipe here when the pins land. -->
  </domain-config>

  <!--
    stage (.env.stage) — the env file marks both of these as PLACEHOLDERS, pending the ingress
    host strategy. They are listed so the config cannot be the reason a stage build is unpinned,
    but do not pin them until the hosts themselves are confirmed.
  -->
  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">velchat-api-gateway.onrender.com</domain>
    <!-- No <pin-set> until a real pin exists: an EMPTY one enforces nothing at runtime and
         Android lint treats it as a fatal error in a release build, so the app could not be
         assembled at all. Paste the pin-set from the header recipe here when the pins land. -->
  </domain-config>
  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">velchat-realtime-gateway.onrender.com</domain>
    <!-- No <pin-set> until a real pin exists: an EMPTY one enforces nothing at runtime and
         Android lint treats it as a fatal error in a release build, so the app could not be
         assembled at all. Paste the pin-set from the header recipe here when the pins land. -->
  </domain-config>

  <!--
    Debuggable builds additionally trust user-installed CAs.

    This is what makes the control TESTABLE: with a proxy CA installed you can watch a debug
    build's traffic, and once pins are real you can prove a release build refuses the same proxy.
    It applies only when android:debuggable is true, so no shipped APK is affected, and per the
    platform's rules these anchors also override pins — which is the point, since a proxy
    certificate could never match a pin.
  -->
  <debug-overrides>
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="user" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </debug-overrides>

</network-security-config>
